  "crypto-market-structure-after-midterms": {
    "text": "Skip to article Independent Market Notes · Policy & Digital Assets Washington · Digital asset policy After the Midterms, CLARITY’s Hardest Vote Is Still Ahead Democrats’ wins reshape the next Congress. They do not turn a stalled crypto market-structure bill into a lame-duck certainty. By Jack Smack · November 4, 2026 Editorial illustration. The calendar is the story: the outgoing Congress retains the gavel during the lame duck; the incoming Congress inherits the next attempt. Election night changed the political map. It did not change the vote that stopped the Digital Asset Market Clarity Act in the Senate. That distinction is easy to lose in the rush to interpret a new majority. The Associated Press called enough races on November 3 to say Democrats had won control of both chambers. It also called Ohio for Sherrod Brown over Republican Jon Husted. But these were election calls, not final state certifications: Ohio’s election office said its posted results were unofficial while canvassing and certification remained ahead. My read is that the result makes a quick, clean revival of CLARITY less likely, not more. The bill’s immediate fate still rests with the Congress elected in 2024, which retains control until the next Congress convenes in January. Democrats can shape that next Congress’s agenda and committee leadership. They cannot retroactively make the current Senate’s procedural failure disappear. The vote that matters happened before Election Day H.R. 3633, the House-passed Digital Asset Market Clarity Act, moved through the Senate Banking Committee in a substantially revised form. In May, the committee advanced a substitute after bipartisan negotiations. That was meaningful progress, but a committee vote is not a Senate floor coalition. On September 15, the Senate failed to invoke cloture on the motion to proceed. The official floor record lists the vote as 49–50. Cloture is the procedural step that would have allowed the chamber to move toward debate; failing there meant the bill never reached the floor debate and passage stage. The question is no longer simply whether lawmakers agree that digital-asset rules need clarification. It is whether they can agree on the bill’s actual allocation of authority and protections. Committee support showed a negotiating coalition. The failed floor vote showed that it was not yet a governing coalition. The difference between those two things is the difference between a bill that has momentum and one that has a path to enactment. A lame-duck window exists, but it is crowded The outgoing majority still sets the agenda during the post-election session. That creates a theoretical opening for another attempt. Yet no official floor notice or committee schedule I reviewed listed a CLARITY vote for the lame duck. A published return calendar is not a bill-specific commitment. Congress also faces end-of-year work that competes for scarce floor time. The Senate’s tentative 2026 calendar identifies December 18 as a target adjournment date. That is a planning marker, not a promise that lawmakers will finish on that date—or that they will spend the remaining time on crypto legislation. There is a further political complication. If the outgoing majority tries to push the bill through after voters have selected a new majority, incoming lawmakers may have little incentive to bless a text they did not shape. That is an inference about legislative incentives, not a public commitment by either party. The election does not prove that a lame-duck deal is impossible; it makes the deal’s politics harder to read. The next Congress can change the bill’s authorship and its substance When the new Congress organizes in January 2027, control of both chambers is expected to shift to Democrats. That should change which party chooses committee chairs in Senate Banking, Senate Agriculture, House Financial Services, and House Agriculture, subject to the chambers’ organization and committee assignments. The specific incoming chairs and ranking members were not established by election-night results. Nor does party control by itself tell us what any committee will do with crypto policy. Members’ votes and statements on stablecoin yield, decentralized finance, customer safeguards, and the SEC–CFTC boundary are more informative than a simple partisan label. The evidence assembled here does not support a complete member-by-member profile of the new committees. Text is likely to be part of the negotiation. The Senate committee advanced a substitute to the House-passed bill, so the chambers were already working from different versions. If lawmakers restart the effort in 2027, they may revisit how digital commodities are defined, which activities receive special treatment, what obligations apply to DeFi developers and intermediaries, and how enforcement authority is divided between the SEC and CFTC. Those are pressure points, not predictions of specific amendments. What is known: The House passed its bill; Senate Banking advanced a substitute; the Senate then failed to move forward procedurally. What remains unknown: whether leaders will schedule another vote, whether a new majority will reuse the same text, and whether the unresolved policy disputes can be closed. Three paths, with the uncertainty kept visible I assign a 5–15% chance to lame-duck passage, a 55–70% chance that the effort slips into 2027 with revised text, and a 20–35% chance that it stalls without near-term enactment. These are editorial probability ranges, not outputs from a statistical model. They are conditional on the evidence available on November 4, 2026, and should move if leadership schedules floor action or sponsors publish a negotiated agreement. PathMy rangeWhat would move it Lame-duck passage5–15%A bill-specific floor schedule, leadership backing, and a demonstrated Senate coalition before adjournment. 2027 restart with revised text55–70%New committee leadership, renewed sponsor negotiations, and public text addressing the disputes that blocked floor progress. Stall20–35%No committee action in the new Congress, competing legislative priorities, or continued disagreement over jurisdiction and DeFi obligations. The ranges are deliberately broad because the key evidence is missing: a published lame-duck schedule, a new cross-party agreement, and a confirmed list of incoming committee leaders. Without those, precision would be theatre. What would change my mind A formal Senate notice scheduling the bill would raise the lame-duck scenario. So would sponsor statements backed by actual vote commitments and a House plan to accept the Senate text. Conversely, explicit leadership opposition, a crowded end-of-year schedule, or a decision to defer substantive work to the next Congress would push the 2027 path higher. For the 2027 scenario, the decisive test is not whether new committee chairs use favorable language about innovation or consumer protection. It is whether they publish text, hold hearings or markups, and build a coalition that can survive a floor vote. Until then, committee control is an opportunity to set priorities—not evidence that a particular crypto rulebook is coming. The result changes leverage, not the legislative math Democratic control of the next Congress will reshape oversight and agenda-setting. It may also make the industry negotiate over a different balance of market access, investor protection, and agency authority. That is a material political shift for a sector that has treated comprehensive market-structure legislation as a defining federal policy goal. But the immediate legislative math remains stubborn. The September cloture vote failed; the lame-duck calendar is limited; and the incoming majority has not yet shown what text it would support. The election changed who gets to write the next chapter. It did not write the chapter for them. Sources and record Associated Press, “Voters give Democrats control of Congress in rebuke to Trump”, November 3, 2026. Accessed November 4, 2026. Associated Press, “Which Senate, governors races are still uncalled by The Associated Press”, November 4, 2026; reports race-call status as of noon Eastern. Accessed November 4, 2026. Ohio Secretary of State, Voting in Ohio / November 3, 2026 election information. Accessed November 4, 2026. U.S. Senate, Senate Floor Activity, September 15, 2026, official roll-call record on H.R. 3633. Accessed November 4, 2026. U.S. Senate, Cloture Motions, 119th Congress, entry for H.R. 3633. Accessed November 4, 2026. Senate Banking Committee, “Senate Banking Committee Advance CLARITY Act in Historic Bipartisan Vote”, May 2026. Accessed November 4, 2026. U.S. Senate, 2026 Senate calendar. Accessed November 4, 2026. Election calls are not state certifications. Scenario probabilities are the author’s judgment, not a forecast issued by a public institution. This article discusses legislative prospects and does not provide investment advice. At a glanceThe Senate procedural hurdle already failed in September. The outgoing Congress keeps control during the lame duck.What to watchBill-specific floor notices, sponsor commitments, the December adjournment calendar, and the new Congress’s committee assignments.",
