    "blocks": [
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A timestamp correction rescued a plausible derivatives signal from look-ahead bias. It did not show that open interest improves on price momentum."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "November 4, 2026 · Historical research, not a live market signal"
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Conceptual editorial artwork. The two lines do not represent recorded market prices or open interest."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A market signal can look convincing for two very different reasons. It can contain information that prices have not yet absorbed. Or it can appear to work because the test quietly gives it information from the future. Bitcoin’s open-interest story sits uncomfortably close to that boundary."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The familiar interpretation is seductive: when Bitcoin rises and futures open interest rises with it, new leveraged positions may be reinforcing the move. When open interest falls, perhaps the rally is merely traders closing shorts. That language gives us a tidy narrative about conviction, positioning and the quality of a trend."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "But a narrative is not a tradeable edge. What matters is whether the extra variable improves a decision relative to a rule that just watches price—and whether the data were genuinely available when that decision would have been made."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In the historical OKX BTC-USDT perpetual dataset examined here, open interest did not clear that bar. An initially troubling timestamp mismatch was substantially reconciled. A modestly profitable hypothetical price-plus-OI strategy survived that correction. Yet a simpler price-momentum strategy still earned higher modeled returns in both available comparison periods."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Open interest counts outstanding derivatives contracts. It is not a direct measure of bullishness: every contract has a long and a short. The interpretation of rising OI depends on who is initiating risk, the direction of price, funding conditions and the structure of the market. It also depends, less romantically, on which clock the database uses."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The original daily and hourly observations for the same instrument initially appeared irreconcilable. At matching printed timestamps, BTC-denominated daily OI differed from hourly OI by an average of about 754 BTC. The daily USD-denominated OI values also differed from BTC OI multiplied by the contemporaneous mark by as much as 6.69%. These were warnings, not evidence of manipulation or a market event."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The revised historical panel supplied a more defensible explanation for the first mismatch. Its hourly OI records distinguish an aggregation bar's printed timestamp from an inferred value time one hour later. Once completed daily OI values were compared to hourly OI on that basis, 58 of 59 comparable daily observations matched exactly; the remaining observation was nearly identical, with a maximum relative difference of roughly 0.0000416%. The corrected hourly BTC OI values also reproduced the earlier hourly series across all 1,439 completed records."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "That reconciliation is substantial, but it has a boundary. It demonstrates agreement within the supplied dataset under a particular time convention. It does not independently prove the provider’s publication latency, and it does not explain every currency- or contract-denominated discrepancy. The smaller gap between reported BTC OI and contracts multiplied by the stated 0.01 BTC multiplier reached about 3.14 BTC."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "This changes how the earlier daily test should be read. The day-level result cannot be treated as a clean, point-in-time predictive experiment once the OI value-time ambiguity is acknowledged. The hourly comparison is more defensible because it deliberately waits: the price candle and corresponding OI interval must finish, and the modeled entry comes at the next hour’s open."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The hourly setup used 1,440 consecutive price observations, 1,439 completed OI observations and 280 funding settlements, with a 24-hour holding period. The rule was deliberately simple: consider a long after both the preceding one-hour price change and the corresponding BTC-denominated OI change are positive. Entries were restricted to nonoverlapping daily opportunities, with a further hour allowed between signal observation and entry. Recorded settlement funding was included; the assumed trading friction was nine basis points per round trip."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Over 40 eligible development opportunities from September 6 through October 15, 2026, the price-plus-OI rule generated ten entries and a compounded hypothetical net return of +6.52%. Over 19 later opportunities from October 16 through November 3, it generated five entries and returned +1.19%. On their own, those positive numbers might seem to vindicate the OI thesis."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Then comes the necessary comparison: what if the same rule ignored open interest and used price direction alone?"
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "OKX BTC-USDT-SWAP; compounded hypothetical strategy returns, unleveraged; recorded funding included and assumed 9 bps round-trip costs deducted. Periods are historical, not forecasts."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The development segment has 40 eligible opportunities; the later segment has 19. Signals triggered 10/5 times for price + OI and 21/11 times for price alone. The later segment was already examined during the wider research process and is not an untouched out-of-sample holdout. Different trading frequencies imply different cost burdens; the repeated daily-long strategy is not continuous buy-and-hold. Source: supplied OKX research datasets and their accompanying reproducible numerical validation, frozen November 4, 2026."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The momentum-only baseline came out ahead in both segments: by roughly 2.79 percentage points in development and 0.60 points later. In these windows, filtering for rising OI reduced exposure, but it did not improve the modeled compounded return compared with simply following positive price momentum."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Both segments favor the simpler price-only rule on modeled return. The two groups are not independent evidence of forward predictive performance. Underlying values and assumptions are provided in the table above."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The distinction is central. There is a coherent story for why open interest could help: a directional price move accompanied by expanding leverage might carry information about the market’s willingness to add risk. But there is also a coherent opposing story. Crowded leverage may be fragile, the sign of open interest says nothing about who is right, and the filter may discard profitable price moves for no compensating benefit. The comparison in this dataset favors the simpler explanation, while remaining too small to settle the general question."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The backtest incorporated 8-hour funding settlements instead of treating funding as a single arbitrary daily adjustment. For the price-plus-OI rule, the total modeled return was +7.48% in development before trading fees and slippage and +1.64% in the later period. Deducting nine basis points per round trip reduced those figures to +6.52% and +1.19%; raising assumed costs to 20 basis points reduced them again to +5.36% and +0.63%."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Price ↑ / OI ↑ rule only. Compounded hypothetical return with recorded funding; 0, 9 or 20 basis points of total assumed round-trip costs."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Costs are scenarios, not measured fills. Funding is based on recorded historical settlement rates. The calculation does not establish realized spread, queue position, market impact, adverse selection, liquidation costs or the effect of leverage."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It is tempting to regard positive results at 20 basis points as proof of robustness. I would not. These are modeled entries and exits at reference prices, not evidence that a trader could consistently obtain those prices at the intended size. And the more frequently a baseline trades, the more often a flat per-trade cost penalizes it. That makes execution design part of the comparison, not a footnote."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "There is a further statistical problem. A profitable segment does not tell us whether the difference from a simpler baseline is meaningful when the OI rule makes only ten trades in one period and five in another. No reliable claim of incremental statistical significance follows from counts that small. Nor can the later segment be renamed a pristine holdout simply because it appears after the first segment on a chart: it was already exposed to previous research decisions."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Open interest remains useful market context. It can describe the extent of outstanding risk and, alongside funding, liquidation activity and order-book depth, help distinguish superficially similar price moves. The error is to turn that contextual value into an untested claim of predictive power."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It matters, too, that the instrument here is a single venue’s BTC-USDT perpetual swap, not the whole Bitcoin market. Exchange-specific position growth can reflect hedging, basis trades or venue shifts as readily as directional speculation. Without independently establishing the identity, timestamp and aggregation of each observation, broad claims about “Bitcoin leverage” become fragile."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The strongest counterargument to the negative result is that the rule is too crude. A binary increase in price and OI ignores the magnitude of the changes, volatility regime, spot flows, the level of funding, basis and where leverage is concentrated. A more carefully specified model might extract something useful. But greater complexity also opens the door to overfitting; the burden of proof rises, not falls."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "What would change my assessment is straightforward: a prespecified signal tested on genuinely new observations collected after the November 4, 2026 research freeze; timestamped OI values whose availability is independently confirmed; comparable execution and funding assumptions for every strategy; and an improvement over price momentum that survives realistic costs, alternative regimes and uncertainty estimates. No such forward observations are contained in the supplied files."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The most instructive result is not that a BTC derivatives strategy made 6.52% in one modeled window. It is that the apparent sophistication of the extra variable did not translate into a better comparison. Once timing was taken seriously and a plain momentum benchmark was put beside the filtered rule, the story became less exciting—and more informative."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "That is where I land: open interest can deepen a market explanation without deserving a place in the trading decision. Until it proves otherwise against a simpler alternative on genuinely unseen data, its proper role is a question to investigate, not an answer to trade."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The historical research covers OKX BTC-USDT-SWAP hourly prices from September 5 through November 4, 2026, the reconciled hourly open-interest series, and 280 recorded 8-hour funding settlements from August 3 through November 4, 2026. Results are as of the November 4, 2026 data freeze; they are not live market conditions. The test uses BTC-denominated OI changes and historical reference-price execution, rather than actual trade fills."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The development window contains 40 eligible daily entry opportunities, September 6–October 15; the later diagnostic window contains 19, October 16–November 3. Signals require positive changes in both one-hour price and OI; modeled entry is one hour after the information becomes available, with a fixed 24-hour hold. Positive funding means a long position pays. Long-only returns use unleveraged compounding, with cash when the rule is inactive. No uncertainty interval or significance claim is asserted."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Source attribution: supplied historical research datasets identified as originating from OKX public market data, plus the associated numerical validation. The reporting conventions can be compared with the official OKX API documentation; this general documentation link is not proof of the precise hourly value-time semantics. Exact data-vintage and response-retrieval timing were not independently certified. The earlier daily-frequency result is excluded from affirmative evidence because of unresolved point-in-time timing."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Illustration and embedded bar chart serve different purposes: the hero is conceptual art; the comparison chart uses the exact rounded hypothetical returns reported in the research. This article is an analytical examination of historical data, not a recommendation or an assurance of strategy performance."
      }
    ],
