    "blocks": [
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "AERO is testing the boundary between an ordinary post-launch shakeout and a deeper repricing. The chart is beginning to stabilize, but the economics tell a more complicated story."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A successful token upgrade is not the same thing as a successful market reaction. That distinction matters for AERO, the token at the center of the Aerodrome–Velodrome combination. The technology can migrate as planned, the exchanges can process their conversions, and the price can still fall. In fact, those things can happen at the same time."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "On October 26, AERO traded around $0.68, roughly 31% below its October 11 peak near $0.993. The new Aero token had launched four days earlier, and major exchange conversions were still ahead. On the one-hour chart, a persistent decline had begun to flatten around $0.65–$0.70. That was the tempting part of the picture: the possibility that sellers were running out of energy."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "My interpretation is more cautious. The selloff may be slowing, but the market has not yet demonstrated that buyers can sustain higher prices. And the chart is only half the problem. Behind the candles, trading activity and protocol revenue are moving in different directions."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The most revealing feature of the short-term chart is not a particular indicator. It is the sequence of failed recoveries. Each rally runs into sellers before it can repair the preceding damage. Price makes lower highs, breaks previous lows, then tries again from a weaker position. It resembles a staircase descending one step at a time."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "At the far right of the October 26 one-hour screenshot, that pattern begins to change slightly. Price has bounced repeatedly from the mid-$0.60s. One attempt reaches toward $0.70 before fading back to approximately $0.68. Buyers are appearing, but they are not yet dictating the direction."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "AERO, one-hour chart, October 26 screenshot. Cropped to isolate the actual price candles and nearby trend overlays; distant annotated levels and trading-account controls are excluded. The crop is an illustration of structure, not a full price-axis reference."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "For a nontechnical reader, there are three prices worth remembering. $0.65–$0.66 is the area buyers have recently defended. $0.69–$0.70 is where the latest rebound ran into resistance. And $0.741–$0.768 is the larger broken support zone that would need to be recovered before I would take a trend reversal seriously."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Breaking above $0.70 would be an improvement. It would not erase the downtrend. A stronger signal would involve a higher low—meaning the next pullback stops above the previous bottom—followed by a sustained reclaim of the $0.74–$0.77 area."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The weekly and daily charts are not saying the same thing. The longer-term uptrend from the February 2026 low around $0.272 was still technically intact in the October 26 analysis. Weekly momentum, however, had weakened. On the daily chart, the damage was clearer: previous swing lows near $0.764–$0.768 had broken, price was below important short-term averages, and momentum had turned bearish."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The four-hour chart offered the earliest hint of relief. Its RSI stood near 34.8, indicating a market that had been sold aggressively, and its MACD histogram had begun to improve. Neither observation is a reversal signal by itself. An oversold market can become more oversold; momentum can improve while price continues to make lower lows."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Four-hour view: the near-term decline and the first signs of momentum easing. Source: October 26 technical-analysis attachment."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Daily view: the failure of the October swing structure. The final daily candle was incomplete at the time of the analysis."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Weekly view: a longer trend that had not yet broken, despite deterioration at shorter horizons."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The $0.66–$0.69 band was not chosen simply because it is a round-number neighborhood. In the October 26 study, several measures converged there: retracement levels from the prior rally, the lower daily Bollinger band near $0.6635, and a substantial historical trading-volume node near $0.6701. That overlap can attract attention and produce temporary support."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "But a crowded level is not a guarantee. The daily average true range was approximately $0.0725, more than 10% of the token's price. In a market that volatile, a few cents of movement is ordinary noise. I would be wary of treating a brief intraday dip below $0.66—or a quick bounce above $0.70—as definitive evidence of anything."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If the current floor gives way on a sustained basis, the next reference area in the attached analysis was around $0.6195, followed by roughly $0.585–$0.60. If buyers recover $0.741–$0.768 and then establish higher lows, the immediate bearish reading would weaken materially. Those are observation points, not promised destinations."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Price levels and volume profile: historically busy price areas can matter, but they do not guarantee support or resistance."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It would be easy to write this story entirely around the merger. The more interesting question is whether the combined protocol is capturing more economic value from its activity. On the evidence available October 26, that answer was not straightforward."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "DefiLlama figures cited in the attached analysis put the combined Aero parent at $16.23 billion in DEX trading volume over 30 days, with $11.10 million in fees and $7.70 million in revenue. Those are meaningful amounts of activity. Yet the direction of change is what catches my attention."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Average daily trading volume increased from about $463 million in the preceding 30-day period to $541 million in the latest one. Average daily fees moved the other way, from $609,000 to $370,000. Average daily revenue also declined, from $491,000 to $257,000."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Percentage changes calculated from the rounded daily averages supplied in the October 26 technical-analysis attachment. Provider: DefiLlama, as reported in that snapshot. Different fee tiers, incentives, pool mix, accounting boundaries and incomplete migration may affect interpretation."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "There are plausible explanations. Lower-fee pools can attract more transactions while earning less per dollar traded. Liquidity was actively shifting from legacy Aerodrome pools into new Aero pools. Some revenue changes may be temporary or reflect a change in how activity is classified. But none of those possibilities should be mistaken for proof that the decline is harmless."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The bull case needs more than impressive gross volume. It needs evidence that the new structure can convert activity into durable value for the token's economic participants."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The new Aero contract launched on October 22, with old AERO converting one-for-one and VELO converting at 0.044 AERO per token. The source material reported roughly 2.129 billion new AERO tokens in total supply and approximately 980 million staked as sAERO—about 46%—at its October 26 observation time."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "That staking share is relevant because it distinguishes tokens committed to the system from those potentially available to trade. It is not, however, a direct measure of the actual sellable float. The available circulating-supply estimates differed by definition, and holder concentration was not independently established. I would not build a precise valuation argument on an unresolved denominator."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The stated emissions framework also deserves patience. The project's published materials described an expected rate around 12% annually, subject to a ceiling, while an early post-launch communication pointed to a much lower initial annualized rate. A single day of observations cannot establish a sustainable issuance regime."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "By October 26, the protocol launch had happened, but exchange conversions were still approaching. Coinbase's conversion window was scheduled for November 2–4, 2026; Binance's old-token deposit and withdrawal suspension was scheduled for November 3. These are operational milestones, not automatic price catalysts."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "During a conversion, some venues may restrict transfers while others continue trading. That can interrupt arbitrage, temporarily widen spreads or create apparent price discrepancies between legacy and new token representations. A price spike during such a window may be driven by market plumbing rather than a durable change in demand."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "My working rule is simple: the quality of a post-migration rally matters more than its size on the day. Does the price hold after trading normalizes? Do higher lows emerge? Does volume persist after the event? Do fee and revenue trends improve? Those are harder tests than a single green candle."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Polygon's MATIC-to-POL transition in September 2024 and Render's RNDR-to-RENDER migration in July–August 2024 show why token conversions need careful event dating. Polygon's protocol upgrade and later exchange-market opening were separate milestones. Render's exchange snapshot and completed conversion were also separated in time. The Artificial Superintelligence Alliance's 2024 AGIX/OCEAN-to-FET consolidation is structurally relevant because it involved more than a straightforward ticker replacement."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "These examples offer mechanisms, not a reliable numerical forecast. An attempted event study sought BTC-relative returns at 30, 14 and seven days before the event, and one, seven, 14 and 30 days after it, along with maximum drawdown and recovery time. The necessary continuous price series could not be validated across the old and new instruments. The resulting historical return cells were left unverified rather than filled with estimates."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "That is an important limit. It would be misleading to claim that comparable migrations historically produce a particular average rebound, or that AERO has a measurable probability of recovering within two weeks. The available evidence does not support those statistics."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Relative strength matters: the attached October 26 analysis showed recent AERO underperformance against BTC and ETH, despite stronger longer-window relative performance."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Relative strength is especially useful here. If AERO rallies only because the entire crypto market rises, that is less convincing than AERO gaining against Bitcoin while its own structure improves. The attached data showed AERO lagging BTC and ETH over seven and 30 days, even though longer windows still reflected earlier leadership."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Support around $0.65–$0.66 survives, the next pullback forms a higher low, and price pushes through $0.70. A sustained reclaim of $0.741–$0.768, ideally with stronger buying participation, would make the recovery thesis more credible."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Repeated attempts at $0.70 fail and price establishes itself below $0.65. The market then has reason to test lower historical trading areas around $0.62 and $0.585–$0.60. That would not automatically destroy the weekly trend, but it would extend the daily correction."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Price oscillates between the mid-$0.60s and the mid-$0.70s while the exchange migration finishes. This could be constructive if selling pressure gradually diminishes, but it would also tie up attention without resolving the trend."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "I would become less skeptical if price built higher lows, regained the broken daily levels, strengthened relative to BTC, and the new pools began translating volume into stronger revenue. Conversely, persistent fee deterioration or renewed lower lows would weaken the stabilization case."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Derivatives do not settle the argument. The October 26 snapshot showed about $25.8 million of AERO open interest on Hyperliquid and roughly $41 million across several visible venues, with positive but moderating funding. Important venues were missing from the sample. It is not enough to claim that a broad short squeeze is imminent, nor enough to rule one out."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Drawdown and volatility: the scale of AERO’s historical swings is a reminder that stabilization can be violent and uneven."
      },
      {
        "tag": "figcaption",
        "text": "Full available daily history in the attachment begins February 6, 2024; it is not the token’s complete lifetime price history."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "What stands out to me is the mismatch between how easy the bullish narrative is to tell and how demanding its proof should be. A larger protocol, new token architecture, broader network footprint and an exchange migration are all substantial developments. None requires the market to assign the token a higher price immediately."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The October 26 chart showed a market trying to find its footing near $0.68. That is worth watching. It is not yet a reversal. The economics showed more trading activity but less revenue per day. That deserves explanation. And the forthcoming exchange conversion introduces another reason to distrust the first dramatic move."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "I would rather be late to a genuine recovery than early to a story that has not yet appeared in the price. AERO's next chapter will be written not by the fact that the merger happened, but by whether the combined system produces stronger economics—and whether buyers can finally defend a higher price."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "All price levels and metrics are historical observations from the October 26, 2026 research snapshot and the supplied one-hour chart. They should not be interpreted as current quotes. The attached technical study used Coinbase daily prices, Binance four-hour prices, exchange quotes, CoinGecko, GeckoTerminal, DefiLlama and project documentation. The latest daily candle was incomplete."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Aero — upgrade and launch hub; conversion terms and project migration information."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Aero — economics dashboard; project-reported staking and economic framework."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Aero — security documentation; contract identity."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Coinbase — token migrations; exchange conversion guidance."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Binance — AERO contract-swap announcement; exchange migration schedule as reported in the October 26 research snapshot."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "DefiLlama; 30-day volume, fee and revenue figures cited in the snapshot."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "CoinGecko and GeckoTerminal; contemporaneous token and pool pricing cross-checks."
      },
      {
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Polygon, Render Network, and Artificial Superintelligence Alliance; historical migration context. No validated quantitative event-return distribution is asserted."
      },
      {
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Comparisons between token migrations are illustrative, not a matched statistical sample. Derivatives coverage and holder concentration were incomplete. Supply figures differ across definitions; preliminary inflation observations should not be annualized into confident forecasts. The charts are retained from the dated source analysis and are not live-updating."
      }
