  "weft-first-week": {
    "text": "Independent Market NotesDigital assets / Investigations Solana · Tokenomics · Market structure WEFT’s first week: who owns the float, who controls the exit? Weft Finance has a functioning market, a public tokenomics story and a strikingly concentrated supply. The harder questions are about what the ledger proves—and what it doesn’t. October 23, 2026Data examined through October 23, 2026Research essay In this pieceThe ownership problemLiquidity and the exitA violent first weekThe buyback questionWhat the chain can proveThe larger lesson In this piece — jump to sectionThe ownership problemLiquidity and the exitA violent first weekThe buyback questionWhat the chain can proveThe larger lesson A token can have thousands of holders and still be a concentrated market. It can report tens of thousands of dollars in liquidity and still be difficult to exit at size. And it can advertise on-chain buybacks without yet offering enough evidence to reconcile every dollar of eligible fees to a purchase or burn. That tension is the real story of Weft Finance (WEFT), a Solana token that entered the market on October 17, 2026. The project describes a product intended to turn trading ideas into automated rules, with some fees directed toward WEFT purchases, distributions and burns. Its premise is not inherently implausible. Its token, however, has to survive a different test: the accounting, ownership and execution realities of an extremely young on-chain market. My interpretation is that the most useful question is not whether WEFT looks bullish or bearish on a chart. It is whether the observable evidence is strong enough to support the story investors might be tempted to tell about it. By the end of its first week, the answer was mixed—and the gaps were consequential. 2,206Reported holder accounts36.56%Top 10 share of supply66.49%Top 40 share of supply Data boundary. These holder figures come from CoinGecko/GeckoTerminal token information dated October 23, 2026, 15:52:57 UTC. They are a historical snapshot, not live readings. The 66.49% figure is the sum of the provider’s top-10, ranks 11–20 and ranks 21–40 categories. [1] Forty accounts, two-thirds of the supply The headline holder count—2,206—sounds encouraging until it is placed beside the distribution. CoinGecko’s October 23 snapshot put 36.5583% of reported WEFT supply in the ten largest listed accounts, another 14.5664% in ranks 11–20 and 15.3668% in ranks 21–40. Only 33.5085% fell outside the first forty positions. Who held reported WEFT supply · October 23 snapshotTop 10 — 36.56%Ranks 11–40 — 29.93%All others — 33.51%Figure 1. Distribution reported by CoinGecko; address accounts are not the same as independent beneficial owners. Percentages sum to 100% subject to rounding. [1] The largest listed address held approximately 73.47 million tokens, or 7.7473% of supply. The second held about 71.20 million, or 7.5080%. Taken together, just two addresses represented roughly 15.26% of all reported WEFT. That matters more than a simple count of wallets. A concentrated float can produce sharp price dislocations even if the broader holder base is expanding. Yet the obvious next step—declaring those addresses an insider syndicate—would get ahead of the facts. Wallet clustering is not identity verification. Shared funding, same-timestamp purchases, direct transfers and exchange withdrawals can be informative, but none automatically proves that the same individual controls every address. Independent scanner narratives illustrate the problem. One report alleged extreme concentration in coordinated launch buying; another characterized the token as having no major insider or bundle flags. A separate October 22 wallet-analysis snapshot said roughly 41.9% of supply was held in one group of wallets linked by funding or transfers, explicitly acknowledging that these links did not prove common ownership. Those conclusions cannot simply be averaged into a credible insider percentage. Their definitions and underlying graphs need to be compared. [2][3] Concentration is observable. Coordination is a hypothesis. Common control requires a stronger chain of evidence.My interpretation of the holder data There is an additional attribution issue around the developer. CoinGecko reported a developer-holding percentage of 3.72% and identified the launch-related address jaoKY…Absu1. Other scanners reported a zero creator-wallet balance at different observation times and under their own labels. That is not necessarily a contradiction: “creator wallet,” “developer-linked holdings” and “economic control” are different things. Without token-account tracing and common-control evidence, the percentage should not be used as a definitive measure of insider ownership. [1][2] The exit is narrower than the liquidity headline The token traded across multiple Solana pools, but the reserves were far from evenly distributed. A CoinGecko pool listing from October 23 placed approximately $49,871 in the principal Meteora DAMM v2 WEFT/SOL pool and another $9,817 in the next-largest Meteora pool. Two smaller Meteora markets added roughly $2,897 and $485. Pool (abbreviated)MarketReported reserves24h volumeE5bd…scgxMeteora DAMM v2$49,871$108,08095zN…32d8Meteora$9,817$36,543AJjE…8oARMeteora$2,897$4,7212zhM…qjibMeteora$485$155 Historical provider snapshots, October 23, 2026. The displayed pool values were retrieved at slightly different times and should be understood as approximate, not as a simultaneous executable order book. “Reserves” is not synonymous with quote-side liquidity available at a given slippage tolerance. [1] Those four pools represented about $63,070 in reported reserves, of which around 79% sat in the largest. The first two held about 95%. Several additional listed pools showed negligible volume and implausibly distant nominal prices; including their last-trade quotes as usable capacity would create an illusion of arbitrage or depth. Why do I care about the distinction? Because the reserve figure is an inventory measure, not a promise about the proceeds from a sale. A Meteora DAMM v2 pool and a concentrated-liquidity market can respond differently to the same trade. The active liquidity range, fee structure, exact reserves, routing and momentary market state determine impact. A back-of-the-envelope constant-product calculation is useful as an illustration, but it cannot certify what a WEFT holder would actually receive. Liquidity was moving, not frozen in place The indexed transaction history adds a more concrete fact: liquidity removals and additions did occur. On October 22 at 12:46:26 UTC, the leading pool’s event feed recorded a removal involving about 861,824 WEFT and 2.415 SOL. On October 23 at 14:28:06 UTC, another removal involved about 32,316 WEFT and 0.095 SOL. A later addition at 16:39:43 UTC involved about 27,912 WEFT and 0.092 SOL. [4] These are transaction-linked events, not evidence of an imminent collapse. Liquidity providers can rebalance positions for routine reasons. But the events do undermine any casual assumption that all reported liquidity is permanently inaccessible to its providers. The unanswered question is who can withdraw the dominant positions, and under what authority? A historical scanner’s “unlocked” label is not enough. Position ownership, token custody and withdrawal permissions must be mapped at a specific finalized Solana slot. A first-week chart that punishes false precision WEFT’s first-week price history is dramatic even by the standards of newly launched crypto tokens. CoinGecko returned seven daily token-level OHLCV observations covering October 17–23, although the October 23 observation was an incomplete day. The October 19 daily bar recorded an intraday high near $0.003845. The partial October 23 close was approximately $0.0003265. Measured from that recorded high, the decline was about 91.5%. [1] WEFT daily close · USD per tokenWeft Finance daily closing price observations from October 17 through October 23, 2026Daily closes: October 17 $0.00027742; October 18 $0.00094643; October 19 $0.00170075; October 20 $0.00085643; October 21 $0.00032306; October 22 $0.00044081; October 23 incomplete $0.00032650.$0.0018$0.0009$017 Oct18 Oct19 Oct20 Oct21 Oct22 Oct23 Oct*Figure 2. Historical daily closing observations reported by CoinGecko. October 23 is incomplete. The daily-close line does not show the October 19 intraday high of roughly $0.003845, which is why the high-to-October-23 calculation is much larger than the decline from the highest daily close. [1] The important point is not that one price should be called the “true” top. It is that the selected observation can dramatically change the apparent drawdown. An earlier summary used a high around $0.0017 and implied a smaller peak-to-current loss. A later daily OHLCV result showed a substantially higher intraday print. Without reconstructing the complete trade tape, it is more defensible to describe the latter as the aggregator-reported observed high, not a consensus all-venue executable peak. There are also far too few completed daily observations to claim a validated statistical edge. Six completed days cannot support a meaningful out-of-sample study of momentum, mean reversion, risk-adjusted performance or tail behavior. The price path tells a story about instability and turnover. It does not tell us what comes next. The buyback narrative meets an accounting problem WEFT’s stated tokenomics connect platform fees to token purchases, rewards and burning. That is a testable proposition—and potentially a more interesting one than the chart. A project that converts real product usage into transparent token flows has a clearer economic mechanism than one driven exclusively by attention. But a mechanism on a website is still a claim until its inputs and outputs reconcile. An independent investigation described a treasury wallet receiving approximately 96 SOL in fees, converting around 16 SOL into WEFT and executing 18 burn instructions totaling roughly 8.82 million WEFT. It also linked a reported buyback to about $1,668. Those figures are useful leads, not a complete audit. [5] The simple ratio—16 SOL of purchases divided by 96 SOL of reported fees—is about 16.7%, well below an advertised headline allocation of half of qualifying platform fees. That is an accounting discrepancy worth pursuing, not proof of wrongdoing. The numerator and denominator may cover different fee types or periods; amounts could remain accrued, reserved or routed through additional wallets. The project has also described separate WEFT trading fees and a buyback component, which should not be confused with an SPL token transfer tax. To settle this properly, I would want every eligible fee receipt tagged by source and time, each treasury transfer, every token purchase, every recipient distribution and each burn instruction, with balances reconciled from one block-pinned state to the next. Without that, a reported decrease in circulating supply cannot be confidently assigned to a specific burn schedule. A provider’s supply revision, a wallet transfer and a genuine token burn are distinct events. A buyback promise becomes economically meaningful only when someone can reconcile the whole path from earned fee to token purchase to final destination.The standard I would apply There is a serious counterargument here. The project may be doing exactly what it describes while public interfaces lag the transactions or independent summaries mismatch categories. Indexed evidence of burn instructions is directionally consistent with a real mechanism. Equally, visible burns do not prove that the full policy has been met. Both propositions can be true at once. The boundary between an explorer record and native-chain proof One part of the evidence deserves particular care: the difference between reconstructing a plausible transaction history and verifying it at identified finalized Solana slots. The creator-linked launch transaction has an explorer-reported time of October 17, 2026, at 17:58:15 UTC. Public indexers also expose transaction signatures for the October 22 and 23 liquidity changes. Those signatures make the claims falsifiable. They can be looked up and decoded. But the available research ledger did not establish the corresponding finalized slots and block hashes, nor did it exhaustively enumerate Meteora position accounts, holder funding paths or treasury instructions. [4][6] The distinction is technical but essential. A displayed block height is not automatically the slot identifier. A transaction appearing in an explorer is not itself an independent proof of the state root or finality commitment. To attribute LP control, a proper reconstruction must inspect the position authority accounts and custody paths, not simply the account that submitted a removal. To attribute common ownership, it must distinguish economically meaningful wallet-to-wallet financing from routine interactions with exchanges, routers and programs. The same caution applies to the candidate fee treasury identified in third-party reporting. An address label is a hypothesis about purpose or control. Without decoded native instructions and conservation checks, a series of transfers cannot be certified as the complete flow of fee income to burns. Evidence threshold. No finalized-slot-complete reconstruction is established by the available source record. This article does not assert that LP controllers, wallet beneficiaries or full fee flows have been independently verified. That missing proof is a substantive limitation, not a footnote. What would actually change the assessment? Three findings would matter most. First, independently verifiable control and withdrawal rights for the main liquidity positions. Second, a funding graph that tests whether the largest holders are financially linked in a way that survives alternative explanations. Third, a slot-consistent fee-to-burn ledger that reconciles eligible receipts, purchases, distributions, unspent balances and burned supply. A fourth requirement sits outside the forensic accounting: a realistic pool-level swap simulation. Reported reserves alone cannot establish how much WEFT can be exchanged at a given cost, and no exact finalized-state routing quote for hypothetical $500, $1,000, $3,000 or $5,000 sales was available in the examined record. The verdictThe danger is not uncertainty. It is pretending the uncertainty has already been priced.I do not think the evidence warrants a categorical accusation of fraud. The official mint is identifiable, the main trading pools are identifiable, token authorities were reported disabled, and indexed transactions point to real liquidity activity and possible buyback-and-burn flows. That is more substance than a ticker alone.It also does not justify a confident recovery thesis. At the October 23 historical snapshot, supply ownership was heavily concentrated, active reserves were clustered in just a few pools, and the first-week price history showed a collapse from an extremely volatile early high. The most consequential questions—economic control, withdrawable liquidity and complete fee accounting—remained open.My conclusion is conditional: WEFT is an interesting market-structure case study, not yet an evidence-qualified directional opportunity. What stands out to me is how well WEFT illustrates a wider small-cap crypto problem. Markets have become exceptionally good at displaying price, volume, holder count and tokenomics slogans. They are less good at expressing whether a price is executable, whether wallets are independent, and whether a token’s promised cash-flow mechanism reconciles at the ledger level. The next standard of credible on-chain research should be less interested in impressive dashboards and more interested in identities, units, timestamps and conservation of assets. On that standard, the right conclusion about WEFT is neither celebration nor condemnation. It is a narrower, more useful statement: the risks are visible, while the most important assurances are not yet proved. ◆ Sources and evidence notes CoinGecko / GeckoTerminal, WEFT pool, and WEFT token listing. Token, holders, pool and OHLCV data referenced are dated October 23, 2026. The historical holder update was 15:52:57 UTC. The figures above were obtained from its on-chain data endpoints; public pages may change. Rug.Tools — WEFT token analysis. Scanner conclusions are presented as vendor claims, not independent proof of coordination or beneficial ownership. Whodl — holder and linkage scan, including its dated October 22, 2026 snapshot. Wallet linkage is not verified ownership. October 22 LP-removal transaction; October 23 LP removal; October 23 LP addition. Times and token amounts reflect indexed events; finalized slots have not been independently pinned. Weft Finance official website, revenue interface, and LarpCheck assessment. The latter’s fee, buyback and burn figures are third-party claims awaiting receipt-level reconciliation. Creator-linked launch transaction, October 17, 2026; Solana RPC documentation for finalized commitment, transaction receipts and account-state semantics. This is a historical market-structure analysis based on dated observations and attributable public-source claims. Market figures can change rapidly. No forecast, guaranteed executable price, causal finding about wallet coordination or independently completed finalized-state audit is asserted. Independent Market Notes · Research perspective · WEFT on Solana · October 23, 2026",
